\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{A descriptive two-pathway framework.}
The results suggest execution traces contain two independent signal pathways for verdict prediction, whose interaction explains why complete traces degrade SFT learning.
We present this as a descriptive working hypothesis, not a causal model.

\paragraph{Explicit pathway: diagnostic tokens.}
The diagnostic token ratio $d$ (\S2.1) correlates with accuracy at $r{=}0.949$ ($p{=}0.014$) across five conditions, and all four models achieve ${\geq}$99.33\% under \texttt{exception\_only} with zero variance on Qwen ($\sigma{=}$0.00, 3 seeds).
Although 55.5\% of failures are silent logic errors, \texttt{exception\_only} achieves 100\% fail recall because test assertions generate \texttt{AssertionError} events.
\texttt{ae\_only}, a strict superset, slightly underperforms \texttt{exception\_only} (Table~\ref{tab:ablation}), confirming that additional content introduces noise.

\paragraph{Implicit pathway: terminal execution state.}
Tail-truncated traces achieve 93--97\% accuracy (Table~\ref{tab:tail_cross_model}) despite $d {\approx} 0$.
Tail truncation at ${\geq}$512 tokens captures \texttt{[Line~X]} variable values at final execution steps, which indirectly encode program outcome; below 384 tokens, the globals module dump consumes the entire budget, yielding ${\sim}$73\% ($\approx$ \texttt{no\_trace}).
The +13.8pp phase transition at tail-384$\to$tail-512 marks where the model accesses terminal execution state.

\paragraph{Diagnostic signal dilution in complete traces.}
Both pathways' signals exist in full traces but are buried under thousands of intermediate tokens.
Figure~\ref{fig:training_dynamics} confirms: \texttt{exception\_only} converges within 50 steps, while \texttt{full\_trace} with label-only loss oscillates (82--89\%) without convergence (Appendix~\ref{sec:training_dynamics}); seed variance negatively correlates with diagnostic density.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{training_dynamics}
\caption{Training dynamics: \texttt{exception\_only} converges to 99.83\% by step 150; \texttt{full\_trace} with label-only loss oscillates (82--89\%) without convergence.}
\label{fig:training_dynamics}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Why SFT over regex?}
SFT \texttt{exception\_only} (99.83\%) exceeds the best regex (B5, 99.33\%) by 0.50pp on caught-exception boundary cases (McNemar $p{=}.014$), provides calibrated probabilities (ECE\,=\,0.0027; \S\ref{sec:calibration}), and retains 97.65\% under marker removal vs.\ B5's 28.49\% (\S\ref{sec:nomarker}).

\paragraph{The marginal value of complete traces.}
Source code alone achieves 73--76\% (\texttt{no\_trace}), so traces add at most ${\sim}$27pp.
Complete traces capture only 4--9pp of this headroom (77--82\%), while exception events capture 24--27pp (${\geq}$99.33\%).
That more information degrades performance is the primary finding; it aligns with classical feature selection theory \citep{guyon2003introduction}, the Information Bottleneck \citep{tishby2000information}, and LIMA's \citep{zhou2023lima} demonstration that minimal high-quality data suffices.

\paragraph{Design implications.}
The two-tier hierarchy suggests a practical fallback: extract exception events first; resort to tail-truncated state when no exception is raised.
Tier~2 benefit is model-dependent (Qwen +10.9/+7.2pp; CodeLlama +0.9pp), so trace design should match deployment model capacity.
Whether this framework transfers to other trace-based tasks \citep{gu2024cruxeval,ni2024next} remains open.

\paragraph{Caveats.}
The decomposition (\S\ref{sec:density}) is a preliminary upper-bound estimate on the Qwen family only (CodeLlama and DeepSeek exhibit label-only collapse); $N{=}4$ models is insufficient for a continuous density--accuracy relationship.
HumanEval/MBPP covers only simple assert-based tests; real-world failure modes remain untested.

